 `brief` parses the brief the same way `plan` parses the plan, so the page renders sections rather than raw markdown. In a `Completion`, `evidence[].route` is the `/api/evidence` URL for a reference that names a file under `.reggie/tasks/<slug>/evidence/` and `null` otherwise; `exists` says whether that file is really there (working tree or task branch), so a criterion claiming proof that was never saved is visible as such. `diff` sums the numstat of the task's own commits and leaves `.reggie/` records out, the way `changedFiles` does.

+### `policy`: what the policy would say about a finished task
+
+`policy` is the object `reggie check <slug> --json` prints, for a task that has a live `task/<slug>` branch with a packet on it, and `null` otherwise. It is a **report**: in this version nothing acts on it, the decide form is still how a task is decided, and the page says so above the form. It is computed per request for this one page and never stored, because it is a function of two commits and both move; `GET /api/tasks` never computes it.
+
+```ts
+interface PolicyReport {
+  slug: string;
+  verdict: 'would-pass' | 'refused' | 'not-evaluated';
+  summary: string;                                   // one sentence: what the verdict rests on, or why the task was not evaluated
+  base: { branch: string; commit: string } | null;   // the integration branch and the full id this evaluation read
+  task: { branch: string; commit: string } | null;   // `task/<slug>` (or `origin/task/<slug>`) and the full id read
+  policy: { mode: 'solo'|'team'; plans: PolicyClass; completions: PolicyClass; source: { plans: PolicySource; completions: PolicySource } } | null;
+  risk: { plan: Risk|'unset'; planFiles: Risk; changed: Risk; effective: Risk; changedCount: number; unplanned: string[] } | null;
+  criteria: { n: number; key: string; text: string; status: CriterionStatus; why: string|null; record: ReportRecord|null }[];
+  reviews: { name: string; key: string; outcome: 'pass'|'fail'; record: ReportRecord }[];
+  badLines: { line: number; why: string }[];         // lines of checks.jsonl that are not records
+  gates: { id: GateId; title: string; status: 'pass'|'fail'|'not-checked'; reasons: string[] }[];
+}
+type PolicyClass = 'none'|'low'|'medium'|'high';
+type PolicySource = 'base-commit' | 'mode-default' | 'unreadable';   // the base commit's `policy` block set it; the block did not; the block's value could not be read and the default stood in
+type CriterionStatus = 'pass' | 'fail' | 'missing' | 'stale' | 'no-evidence';
+type GateId = 'policy' | 'plan' | 'controls' | 'packet' | 'criteria' | 'evidence' | 'risk' | 'merge';
+interface ReportRecord { outcome: 'pass'|'fail'; person: string; tool: string; session: string; at: string; head: string; evidence: string[]; note: string }
+```
+
+The evaluation reads exactly two commits, each resolved once to a full id, and no working tree, so the object is byte-identical whether the server runs from the integration checkout or from the task's worktree, whatever is uncommitted in either. From the base commit: `.reggie/config.yaml` (the mode, the `policy` block, the risk rules) and the task's `plan.md` (the criteria, the `risk` line, the file list). From the branch tip: `packet.md`, `checks.jsonl`, the evidence files and the list of changed files. The one thing taken from a disk is the integration branch's name, from the integration checkout's config (a Reggie worktree is `<integration checkout>/.worktree/<slug>`), never from a task worktree's.
+
+`verdict` is `would-pass` when no gate fails (`not-checked` does not fail), `refused` otherwise, and `not-evaluated`, with `gates: []` and the reason in `summary`, when there is nothing to judge: the integration branch cannot be named or its name is not one Reggie will hand to git, a config that does not parse, team mode, a task already approved on the base (naming who decided it and when), no `task/<slug>` branch, or no packet on it. The gates, in order:
+
+- `policy`: fails when `completions` is `none` on the base commit.
+- `plan`: the plan is committed on the base, passes the plan contract, and has at least one criterion. A plan that exists only on the branch fails, because there is then no copy the branch could not have edited.
+- `controls`: fails, naming each path, when the branch changes a file Reggie or the next session obeys: its own `plan.md`, `.reggie/config.yaml`, `.reggie/people.yaml`, anything under another task's folder, `.mcp.json`, `.gitattributes`, or anything under `.claude/`. The list is fixed in code and compared without regard to letter case.
+- `packet`: the packet's verdict is `pending` and it satisfies the packet contract (`reggie packet <slug> --lint`): its own slug, the seven sections filled, a generated checklist equal to what the records render against the base plan, and well-formed citations.
+- `criteria`: every criterion of the base plan has a latest record that passes, cites evidence that resolves, and is not stale; no record of any kind fails (a failing review, or a failing record whose key is in no plan, blocks); no line of `checks.jsonl` is unreadable; and there is a checks file at all: a packet with no checks never passes. `criteria[].status` is `missing` with no record for the key, `fail` when the latest record fails, `no-evidence` when the pass cites none or cites a file that does not resolve, `stale` when the record's `head` is not an ancestor of the branch tip or a file outside `.reggie/` changed after it, else `pass`. The latest record for a key is the last one in the file; the clock is never consulted. Reviews are listed and never required.
+- `evidence`: every citation of the packet and every evidence path of a passing record resolves on the branch tip (the rule is under `POST /api/decide` below).
+- `risk`: `risk.effective`, the highest of the plan's `risk` line, `riskFromFiles` over its file list, and `riskFromFiles` over every path the branch changed since its merge base (listed with rename detection off, so a file moved out of `src/auth/` still counts under its old name; the task's own folder and `.reggie/journal/` are left out), is within `completions`. `risk.unplanned` lists the changed paths the plan never named.
+- `merge`: `git merge-tree --write-tree` merges the two commits in memory without a conflict, naming the conflicted paths when it does not. It touches no checkout. On a git older than 2.38 the gate is `not-checked` and changes no verdict.
+
+Every string that came out of a check record (`person`, `tool`, `session`, `at`, `note`, `evidence`) is cleaned to one line with control and bidirectional characters removed, because a record is a file anyone who can commit on the branch can write; a client must still set them as text and never as markup.
+
+### Check records: `.reggie/tasks/<slug>/checks.jsonl`
+
+One JSON object per line, appended on the task branch by `reggie check` and the `reggie_check` MCP tool, never committed by them: `{ v: 1; kind: 'criterion'|'review'; key: string; n: number; text: string; outcome: 'pass'|'fail'; evidence: string[]; note: string; person: string; tool: string; session: string; at: string; head: string }`. A criterion's `key` is `c:` and the first twelve hex characters of the SHA-256 of its whole bullet in the plan (first line and indented continuation lines, whitespace collapsed), with `#2`, `#3` for identical bullets, so renumbering changes nothing and one changed word makes a new key; a review's is `r:<name>`. `evidence` holds full repo-relative paths of files directly inside the task's evidence folder; `head` is the commit the checkout was on. `n` and `text` are for readers and are never trusted. A line that is not such an object (not JSON, not an object, over 16 KB, a wrong or unknown field) is counted by line number in `badLines` and is itself a refusal. The packet's acceptance checklist is generated from these records between `<!-- reggie:checks:start -->` and `<!-- reggie:checks:end -->` by `reggie packet`; `packet.criteria` reads that block back like any other checklist.
+
